+# Release blocked: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` does not match its published checksum
+
+## Status
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs, which are fixed:
+
+- Artifacts were opened in text mode, so binary files either raised
+  `UnicodeDecodeError` or were hashed after newline re-encoding. They are now
+  read in binary mode.
+- Manifest digests are uppercase while computed digests are lowercase; the
+  comparison is now case-insensitive.
+
+After those fixes, the release check still fails on one artifact:
+
+```
+$ python3 verify.py
+checksum mismatch: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+## Why this cannot be fixed in code
+
+The mismatch is in the artifact data itself:
+
+| | SHA-256 |
+|---|---|
+| `SHA256SUMS` expects | `7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD` |
+| `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` actually has | `FDE9B2575EDBEFB297FA7D93738B4BB1F09B7F777D1723B90194AD53CD9BC9CE` |
+
+`cli-2.0.1.zip` and `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` both match once the verifier bugs are
+fixed. Git history contains only the initial commit, and it holds the same
+4000-byte file, so there is no earlier good copy to restore from. The archive
+is not a real tar/gzip either (it is opaque binary data), so its contents cannot
+be used to rebuild it.
+
+Simple corruption (a stray leading/trailing byte) was ruled out: hashing the
+file with 1–16 bytes removed from either end matches neither the expected value
+nor any other candidate.
+
+Making the check pass by editing `SHA256SUMS` to the observed hash, or by
+otherwise suppressing the mismatch, would defeat the purpose of the release
+checksum: it would publish an artifact whose provenance has not been verified.
+The test `test_release_artifacts_verify` correctly requires the manifest and
+artifacts to agree, so it has not been weakened or skipped.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+One of the following, from a trusted source:
+
+1. **Restore the correct artifact** for `libfoo-1.4.2` from the build/release
+   system and re-run `python3 verify.py`.
+2. **Confirm the manifest is stale**, obtain the corrected SHA-256 from the
+   release owner, and update `SHA256SUMS` through the normal signing/approval
+   process, then re-run the check.
+
+## Verification
+
+With the code fixes in place:
+
+- `python3 -m unittest -v test_verify.py`
+  - `test_binary_files` — PASS
+  - `test_reports_missing` — PASS
+  - `test_release_artifacts_verify` — FAIL only because of the
+    `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` data mismatch above.
